//Static Data Members

A static data member is a variable inside a class that has only one shared copy for the entire class, rather than one copy for each object.

Example:

class Student {
public:
    int age;
    static int count;
};

Here:

age → each object gets its own copy
count → one shared copy for all objects

For example:

Student s1;
Student s2;

s1.age = 20;
s2.age = 21;

age is different for s1 and s2.

But:

Student::count

refers to the same count shared by s1 and s2.

Important

A static data member is not stored separately inside each object.

Think:

s1 → age
s2 → age

s1 ─┐
    ├──→ same count
s2 ─┘

Simple definition:

Static data member = class variable with one shared copy among all objects.

Yes, it can be used for accessing, but more generally :: is the scope resolution operator.

For example:

Student::count

means:

Access count that belongs to the Student class.

But :: is also used when defining a static member outside the class:

int Student::count = 0;

Here it means:

Define the count that belongs to Student.

So remember:

:: = tells C++ which scope/class something belongs to.

For your static-member topic, you'll commonly see it for both accessing and defining.

